# Debugging GUEST code under lldb.
#
#   lldb -s tools/kl_guest.lldb -- ./build/m_boot <target>
#
# The guest is not a dyld image: its libraries are mapped by the runtime's own
# loader, so lldb has no symbols, no image list entry and no way to name a guest
# address. Three things follow, and this file is the first two.
#
# 1. SIGNALS. A guest built for Android ships a Boehm GC that suspends threads
#    for a collection by SIGNAL rather than by mach port. Those arrive
#    constantly. lldb stops on them by default, which makes the session unusable
#    and — because they arrive in bursts — looks convincingly like a breakpoint
#    firing over and over. Pass them through: -n false (do not stop), -p true
#    (deliver, because the guest is waiting for exactly this).
#
# 2. WHERE THE GUEST IS. Every boot prints
#
#        === guest image map (subtract the base to disassemble) ===
#          libunity.so   0x107cd4000 .. 0x109139bc0
#
#    That base is what turns a crash pc, a breakpoint address and a sampling
#    profile into an offset that matches `objdump -d` of the .so. Nothing else
#    in the process knows it, and it changes every run.
#
# 3. WHAT THE FRAMES MEAN. A tail call through a bad function pointer (`br xN`)
#    builds no frame, so `bt` describes an older frame or garbage — and lldb
#    will symbolise a guest address as whatever host symbol is nearest below it,
#    which is a wrong answer that looks like a right one. Trust `$lr` and the
#    runtime's own fault report over `bt` for that class of fault.
#
# The breakpoint below is a TEMPLATE, commented out. Fill in an address computed
# as (base from the image map) + (offset from your disassembly). A condition
# matters more than it looks: a per-frame guest function is hit thousands of
# times a second, so an unconditional breakpoint on one is not a session.
#
#   br set -a 0x107cd4000+0xbf6028 -c '$x0 == 0x300000000'
#
# For a value that is written rather than called, watch it instead — but arm the
# watchpoint from a breakpoint that only fires once the guest is up, because the
# address does not exist before then and a watchpoint on unmapped memory never
# fires and never says so:
#
#   watchpoint set expression -w write -s 8 -- <address>

breakpoint set --name kl_glfb_begin_render_window
run

process handle SIGXCPU -n false -p true -s false
process handle SIGUSR1 -n false -p true -s false
process handle SIGUSR2 -n false -p true -s false

# --- put a `br set` / `watchpoint set` here; the guest is alive and mapped ---

breakpoint delete 1
continue
